CONTACT;X-BEDEWORK-UID=8a0290b4-860465b2-0186-32f30301-00004779:Rice\, Cyn
 thia
CREATED:20260616T150441Z
DESCRIPTION:A 6 eV bandgap crystal used to be a textbook example of an ele
 ctrical insulator.  But today it has become possible to make pn diodes\, 
 transistors\, LEDs\, and lasers with ultrawide bandgap nitride semiconduc
 tors.  In the first part of my talk\, I will discuss how over the last 25
  years\, we have discovered a powerful new tool rooted in the physics of 
 broken symmetry in semiconductor crystals to tame the conductivity of ult
 rawide bandgap semiconductor heterostructures.  I will discuss how this l
 ong and systematic study has now unleashed their remarkable potential to 
 advance the field of semiconductor devices beyond what was imaginable in 
 the 20th century\, ranging from electronics\, photonics to memory.  In th
 e second part of my talk\, I will discuss how we are using these new adva
 nces in semiconductor materials and device physics and high-speed microwa
 ve electronics to enable new advances to the emerging field of devices fo
 r quantum computation\, communication\, sensing\, and metrology.\n\nDebde
 ep Jena is the David E. Burr Professor of Engineering at Cornell Universi
 ty.  His teaching and research are in the quantum physics of semiconducto
 rs and electronic and photonic devices based on quantized semiconductor s
 tructures (e.g. Nitrides\, Oxides\, 2D Materials)\, and their heterostruc
 tures with superconductors\, ferroelectrics and magnets\, with device app
 lications in energy-efficient transistors\, light-emitting diodes and RF 
 and power electronics and quantum computation and communications.  The re
 search from his group has been published in more than 500 journal papers 
 including in Science\, Nature\, Physical Review Letters\, Applied Physics
  Letters and Electron Device Letters and garnered multiple patents.  His 
 group's research has resulted in two spinoff companies (Soctera and Gallo
 x).  He is a fellow of the APS and the IEEE and is the winner of teaching
  awards and research awards such as the ISCS young scientist award in 201
 2\, MBE young scientist award in 2014\, the IBM faculty award in 2012\, a
 nd the Intel Outstanding Research award in 2020.  He is the author of the
  new textbook "Quantum Physics of Semiconductor Materials and Devices".
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20260616T150441Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260617T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260616T150441Z
