CREATED:20260608T203155Z
DESCRIPTION:Nicole Arrato\, PhD is a postdoctoral associate in the Departm
 ent of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences\, Division of Behavioral Medici
 ne. She is a licensed clinical psychologist whose work lies at the inters
 ection of psychology\, oncology\, and aging. Her work aims to develop\, e
 xamine\, and disseminate behavioral interventions to reduce psychological
  and physiological symptoms experienced by older adults with cancer. One 
 of her current lines of work focuses on the comorbidity of cancer and ste
 roid-induced hyperglycemia/diabetes. Her Grand Rounds presentation will p
 resent work on a) the acceptability and feasibility of use of glucose mon
 itoring for patients with breast cancer\, b) patient and provider perspec
 tives on care coordination for patients with this comorbidity\, and c) ne
 xt research steps to address the identified gaps in care coordination.\n\
 nDr. Max Roberts is a licensed clinical psychologist and postdoctoral ass
 ociate with an upcoming appointment to Assistant Professor in the Departm
 ent of Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences at Duke University School of Me
 dicine (DUSOM). Dr. Roberts completed his PhD in clinical psychology at t
 he University at Albany\, SUNY\, his clinical internship at the VA Maryla
 nd Healthcare System\, and postdoctoral fellowship at DUSOM. His research
  is grounded in contextual behavioral science and focuses on identifying 
 and modifying psychological processes that underlie health-interfering be
 haviors\, with a particular emphasis on improving outcomes for individual
 s with type 1 diabetes. He is especially interested in the development an
 d optimization of scalable\, digitally delivered interventions based on A
 cceptance and Commitment Therapy\, aiming to increase access to evidence-
 based care. Dr. Roberts has published in the areas of psychological flexi
 bility\, diabetes distress\, disordered eating\, anxiety\, and digital he
 alth behaviors like problematic smartphone use\, and his work has been su
 pported by competitive funding\, including a current Breakthrough T1D pos
 tdoctoral fellowship. His program of research reflects a commitment to ad
 vancing mechanistically informed\, accessible behavioral interventions wi
 th real-world clinical impact.
DURATION:PT1H
DTSTAMP:20260608T203155Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20260618T120000
LAST-MODIFIED:20260608T203155Z
